WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.344
Verb Tense and Verb Forms

00:00:02.724 --> 00:00:06.825
Match the time clues. Every sentence needs one main verb.

00:00:08.025 --> 00:00:09.023
How to spot it

00:00:09.423 --> 00:00:11.322
One verb in different tenses

00:00:11.872 --> 00:00:13.719
Time clues like dates and “since”

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:17.912
It’s testing: the verb tense the timeline calls for

00:00:19.112 --> 00:00:19.907
The method

00:00:20.307 --> 00:00:22.721
Step 1: Hunt for time clues

00:00:23.001 --> 00:00:26.319
Dates, “since”, “by the time”, other verbs.

00:00:26.869 --> 00:00:29.123
Step 2: Pick the time frame

00:00:29.404 --> 00:00:32.743
Finished past, past-to-now, or earlier past?

00:00:33.293 --> 00:00:35.649
Step 3: Check for a main verb

00:00:35.929 --> 00:00:40.662
Missing? Use a real verb. Already there? Use -ing or “to”.

00:00:41.212 --> 00:00:43.751
Step 4: Keep lists in one tense

00:00:44.031 --> 00:00:45.635
Same tense all the way through.

00:00:46.185 --> 00:00:47.294
In a few words

00:00:47.444 --> 00:00:49.942
Clue. Time frame. Main verb.

00:00:51.142 --> 00:00:51.965
The rules

00:00:52.365 --> 00:00:54.346
A past date = simple past

00:00:54.626 --> 00:00:58.831
Wrong: In 1928, Fleming has noticed the mold.

00:00:59.111 --> 00:01:03.000
Right: In 1928, Fleming noticed the mold.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:04.994
Since = has / have

00:01:05.274 --> 00:01:09.636
Wrong: Since 1945, the UN expanded.

00:01:09.916 --> 00:01:14.647
Right: Since 1945, the UN has expanded.

00:01:15.197 --> 00:01:16.679
Earlier past = had

00:01:16.959 --> 00:01:21.479
Right: By 1519, the Aztecs had built a huge city.

00:01:22.029 --> 00:01:23.725
One main verb per sentence

00:01:24.005 --> 00:01:27.852
Wrong: The study, which surveyed adults, finding that…

00:01:28.132 --> 00:01:31.714
Right: The study, which surveyed adults, found that…

00:01:32.264 --> 00:01:33.929
Authors argue in the present

00:01:34.209 --> 00:01:36.602
Right: Ruiz argues that… She shows…

00:01:37.802 --> 00:01:38.843
Let’s try one

00:01:39.243 --> 00:01:46.307
Before the Erie Canal opened in 1825, shipping a ton of flour from Buffalo to New York City took

00:01:46.307 --> 00:01:51.989
about three weeks. By the time the canal ______ its first full year of operation,

00:01:51.989 --> 00:01:54.469
the same trip took about eight days.

00:01:55.119 --> 00:02:01.045
Time clues: 1825, took, and by the time. It’s all in the past.

00:02:01.596 --> 00:02:05.549
Completes is present. Will complete is future.

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:09.199
Has completed connects to now, not to the 1820s.

00:02:09.749 --> 00:02:14.107
Had completed means finished before a point in the past. Choice C.

00:02:15.307 --> 00:02:16.302
Common traps

00:02:16.702 --> 00:02:17.865
Has vs had

00:02:18.145 --> 00:02:21.823
“Has” connects to now. “Had” points to an earlier past.

00:02:22.373 --> 00:02:23.498
The -ing fragment

00:02:23.778 --> 00:02:25.729
“Finding” can’t be the main verb.

00:02:26.929 --> 00:02:28.673
Verb Tense and Verb Forms

00:02:28.953 --> 00:02:31.451
Clue. Time frame. Main verb.

00:02:32.001 --> 00:02:32.774
Spot it

00:02:32.974 --> 00:02:35.907
One verb in different tenses + time clues

00:02:36.457 --> 00:02:37.165
Steps

00:02:37.365 --> 00:02:39.221
1. Hunt for time clues

00:02:39.501 --> 00:02:41.297
2. Pick the time frame

00:02:41.577 --> 00:02:43.577
3. Check for one main verb

00:02:43.857 --> 00:02:45.873
4. Keep lists in one tense

00:02:46.423 --> 00:02:47.175
Remember

00:02:47.375 --> 00:02:49.849
Past date → simple past (noticed)

00:02:50.129 --> 00:02:51.571
since → has / have

00:02:51.851 --> 00:02:53.333
Earlier past → had

00:02:53.613 --> 00:02:55.176
-ing can’t be the main verb

00:02:55.456 --> 00:02:57.447
Authors argue → present tense

00:02:57.997 --> 00:02:58.602
Trap

00:02:58.802 --> 00:03:01.688
has (up to now) vs had (earlier past)

